/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Simple levelling uses one set-up: the difference of level between two points both visible from one station is BS − FS.
   - ΣBS is the sum of all backsights and ΣFS the sum of all foresights in the run, in metres.
   - Fly levelling is a quick differential run with long, balanced sights and no intermediate sights, to bring a benchmark's height to a site.
   - The permissible closing error is written e = C√K mm, with K the run length in km and C set by the class of work (a few mm for precise levelling, more for ordinary work).
   - Profile levelling (longitudinal sectioning) finds ground RLs along the centre line of a road, railway, canal or sewer, pegged every 20 m or 30 m and at changes of slope.
   - Cross-sections are levelled at right angles to the centre line at each peg, at set offsets left and right.
*/
